{
  "$schema": "../family.schema.json",
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "elbow-extension",
  "name": "Elbow Extension",
  "definition": "A strict open-chain isolation movement in which the elbow extends while a reviewed upper-arm and forearm posture is held and shoulder, wrist, torso, and lower-body motion do not create the repetition. Triceps is the sole primary on every record: the reviewed shoulder positions inform length, excitation, and adaptation differences but do not justify head-specific categorical roles or a split of the aggregate triceps mesh. The roster admits directly reviewed unilateral supinated and pronated cable pushdowns, bilateral straight-bar pronated and rope neutral-grip pushdowns, standing supinated overhead cable extension, seated overhead dumbbell extension, and supine dumbbell extension. Single handles, dumbbells, and the unreported overhead cable interface remain unilateral; the straight bar and rope are separate bilateral branches. Finger flexors author the static implement hold and extensor carpi radialis supplies mechanics-derived neutral-wrist counter-control. Scapular support, held-forearm control, and stabilizer assignments are bounded mechanics applications rather than variables directly compared in the triceps trials; brachioradialis is assigned only as a forearm stabilizer, never as an elbow-extension mover. The overhead cable source does not report its attachment, pulley height, or cable angle, so that record explicitly preserves an unreported cable interface.",
  "evidenceRefs": [
    "holzbaur-2005-upper-extremity",
    "alves-2018-triceps-shoulder-position",
    "maeo-2023-overhead-neutral-elbow-extension",
    "villalba-2024-pushdown-forearm-position",
    "hussain-2020-triceps-pushdown-fatigue",
    "ace-2026-triceps-pushdown"
  ],
